    96	\section{Introduction}
    97	
    98	
    99	A {\it latin square} of order $q$ is  a $q\times q$ array of $q$
   100	symbols where each symbol occurs exactly once in every row and in
   101	every column.  A $k$-dimensional array satisfying the same condition
   102	is called a {\it latin $k$-cube}. Any $2$-dimensional axis-aligned
   103	plane (face) of a latin $k$-cube of order $q$ is a latin square of
   104	order $q$ by definition. Two latin squares are {\it orthogonal} if,
   105	when they are superimposed, every ordered pair of symbols appears
   106	exactly once. For brevity, a pair of orthogonal latin squares is
   107	called POLS. If in a set of latin squares, any two latin squares are
   108	orthogonal then this set is called a system of Mutually Orthogonal
   109	Latin Squares (MOLS). Two latin $k$-cubes are {\it orthogonal} if
   110	any pair of corresponding $2$-dimensional faces of these cubes is a
   111	POLS. Bose, Shrikhande and Parker \cite{BSP} proved that for each
   112	positive integer $q$, $q\neq 2,6$, there exists  POLS of order $q$
   113	and POLS of orders $2$ and $6$ are not exist. As a corollary we
   114	obtain nonexistence of pairs of orthogonal latin $k$-cubes of orders
   115	$2,6$. A nonexistence of pairs of orthogonal latin $k$-cubes of
   116	orders $q$ if $k>q-1$ follows from the sphere-packing (Hamming)
   117	bound. But the complete spectrum of possible orders of pairs of
   118	orthogonal latin $k$-cubes remains unknown for any $k\geq 3$. Ten is
   119	the minimum order for which it is not known whether  pairs of
   120	orthogonal latin $3$-cubes exist. In this paper we construct pairs
   121	of orthogonal latin $3$-cubes for a sequence  of previously unknown
   122	orders $q_i=16(18i-1)+4$ and $q'_i=16(18i+5)+4$. New pairs of
   123	orthogonal latin $3$-cubes are created as files. These files are
   124	available on the website
   125	https://ieee-dataport.org/open-access/graeco-latin-cubes. The
   126	minimum new order for a pair of orthogonal latin cubes obtained by
   127	proposed construction is $84$.
   128	
   129	Let $Q_q=\{0,\dots,q-1\}$. A subset $C$ of $Q_q^d$ is called an
   130	$MDS(t,d,q)$ {\it code} (of order $q$, code distance $t+1$ and
   131	length $d$) if $|C\cap\Gamma|=1$ for each $t$-dimensional
   132	axis-aligned plane $\Gamma$. Ethier and Mullen \cite{EMullen} proved
   133	that $MDS(2,2+s,q)$ codes are equivalent to pairs of orthogonal
   134	latin $s$-cubes of order $q$. There are two well-known methods for
   135	constructing  MDS codes. If $q$ is a prime power, then we can
   136	consider  $Q_q$ as the Galois field $GF(q)$. MDS codes obtained as
   137	the solution of an appropriate system of linear equations over
   138	$GF(q)$ are known as Reed--Solomon codes. If there exists an
   139	$MDS(t,d,p_1)$ code and an $MDS(t,d,p_2)$ code, then we get an
   140	$MDS(t,d,p_1p_2)$ code by a product construction (McNeish's
   141	theorem). We represent a new construction of $MDS(2,5,q)$ codes that
   142	is similar to  Wilson's construction  for pairs of orthogonal latin
   143	squares with aligned subsquares (see, \cite{DK} and \cite{Zhu}).
   144	
   145	The problem of existence of MDS codes with non-prime-power orders is
   146	 connected to the problem of existence of Steiner block designs. By
   147	methods of random graph theory Keevash \cite{Keevash1} and Glock et
   148	al. \cite{Glock} proved that the natural divisibility conditions are
   149	sufficient for existence of Steiner system $S(t,k,n)$ apart from a
   150	finite number of exceptional $n$ for given fixed $t$ and $k$. It is
   151	not difficult to prove that any MDS code is equivalent to a
   152	transversal in an appropriate multipartite hypergraph (see
   153	\cite{Pot}). Then the existence of  $MDS(t,d,q)$ codes   follows
   154	from   \cite{Keevash3} (Theorem 1.7) apart from a finite number of
   155	exceptional $q$ for given fixed $t$ and $d$. In the last section of
   156	this paper we propose a construction of pairs of orthogonal latin
   157	$3$-cubes based on Steiner block designs.
   158	
   159	Note that an $MDS(2,q+1,q)$ code  (a pair of orthogonal
   160	$(q-1)$-cubes) is an  $1$-error correcting perfect code. The
   161	existence of such codes is a well-known problem if $q$ is not a
   162	prime power (see \cite{MS}).
   163	
   164	
   165	
   166	
   167	
   168	
   169	
   170	\section{Connection between MDS codes and orthogonal systems}
   171	
   172	
   173	
   174	
   175	An ${\rm OA}_\lambda (s,d,q)$  orthogonal array is a $\lambda
   176	q^s\times d$ array whose entries are from $Q_q$ such that in every
   177	subset of $s$ columns of the array, every $s$-tuple from $Q_q^s$
   178	appears in exactly $\lambda$ rows. Further we consider only
   179	orthogonal arrays with $\lambda=1$. In this case every column of the
   180	orthogonal array is a function $f:Q_q^s\rightarrow Q_q$. A set of
   181	columns of an orthogonal array with $\lambda=1$ is called an
   182	orthogonal system. In other words, a system consisting of $d$
   183	functions $f_1,\dots,f_d$, $f_i:Q_q^s\rightarrow Q_q$ ($d\geq s$) is
   184	{\it orthogonal} if for each subsystem $f_{i_1},\dots,f_{i_s}$
   185	consisting of  $s$ functions it holds
   186	$$ \{(f_{i_1}(\overline{x}),\dots,f_{i_s}(\overline{x})) \ |\ \overline{x}\in Q_q^{s}\}=Q_q^{s}.$$
   187	%We assume that a system is orthogonal if $k<s$.
   188	If the system remains orthogonal after substitution any constants
   189	for each subset of variables, then it is called {\it
   190	strong-orthogonal}. If the number of variables is two, then such
   191	system is a system of MOLS (see \cite{EMullen}). If $s=3$, it is a
   192	set of Mutually Orthogonal Latin Cubes (MOLC). Ethier and Mullen
   193	\cite{EMullen} proved that MDS codes are equivalent to
   194	strong-orthogonal systems. Moreover, by a replacement of variables
   195	it is possible to obtain a strong-orthogonal system consisting of
   196	$d-s$ functions from  any orthogonal system consisting of $d$
   197	functions over $Q_q^s$.
   198	
   199	\
   200	\begin{proposition}\label{proEM}
   201	The following conditions are equivalent:\\
   202	1) a system consisting of $t$ functions $f_1,\dots,f_t$,
   203	$f_i:Q_q^s\rightarrow Q_q$  is { strong-orthogonal};\\
   204	2) the set $C=\{(x_1,\dots,x_s,f_1(\overline{x}),\dots
   205	f_t(\overline{x})) : x_i\in Q_q\}$ is an $MDS(t,t+s,q)$ code; \\
   206	3) the array consisting of all elements of $C$ as rows is an ${\rm
   207	OA}_1 (s,t+s,q)$ orthogonal array.
   208	\end{proposition}
   209	
   210	A projection (punctured code) of an $MDS(t,t+s,q)$ code onto a
   211	hyperplane  is equal to a removal of one of the functions $f_i$. The
   212	punctured code  is an $MDS(t-1,t+s-1,q)$ code by Proposition
   213	\ref{proEM}. Consequently, an existence of $MDS(2,2+s,q)$ code or a
   214	pair of orthogonal latin $s$-cubes of order $q$  follows from an
   215	existence of  an $MDS(t,t+s,q)$ code if  $t\geq 2$.
   216	
   217	Sometimes the terms ``latin cube" and ``$t$ mutually orthogonal
   218	latin cubes" is used for  ${\rm OA}_q (2,4,q)$   and ${\rm OA}_q
   219	(2,t+3,q)$  orthogonal arrays respectively. It is easy to see that
   220	our definition of a system of MOLC is stronger.
   221	
   222	
   223	
   224	
   225	\section{Constructions of MDS codes}
   226	
   227	The Hamming distance $\rho$ between two elements of $Q_q^d$ is the
   228	number of positions at which the corresponding symbols are
   229	different. In this paper we use only the Hamming distance. The code
   230	distance of $C\subset Q_q^d$ is $\rho(C)=\min\limits_{x\in C,y\in
   231	C,x\neq y}\rho(x,y)$. The distance between two subsets $A,B\subset
   232	Q_q^d$ is $\min\limits_{x\in A,y\in B}\rho(x,y)$. The Singleton
   233	bound  for the cardinality of a code $C\subset Q_q^d$ with  distance
   234	$t+1$  is $|C|\leq q^{d-t}$.
   235	 MDS codes  achieve equality in this
   236	 bound.
   237	
   238	\begin{proposition}\label{proNM5}
   239	A subset $C\subset Q_q^d$ with code distance $t+1$ is an {\rm MDS}
   240	code if and only if $|C|=q^{d-t}$.
   241	\end{proposition}
   242	
   243	The Hamming bound for the cardinality of code $C\subset Q_q^d$ with
   244	distance $3$ is $|C|\leq \frac{q^d}{1+(q-1)d}$. Then the
   245	inequalities $q^{d-2}\leq \frac{q^d}{1+(q-1)d}$ or $d\leq q+1$ are a
   246	necessary condition for the existence of an $MDS(2,d,q)$ code.
   247	Consequently, an $MDS(2,5,3)$ code or a pair of orthogonal latin
   248	cubes of order $3$ do not exist. Moreover, by puncturing codes we
   249	have a necessary condition  $s\leq q-1$ for the existence of an
   250	$MDS(t,t+s,q)$  if $t\geq 2$. For linear codes this condition $s\leq
   251	q-1$ is in \cite{MS}.
   252	
   253	
   254	
   255	Let $q$ be a prime power and let $Q_q=GF(q)$.  A linear
   256	$k$-dimensional subspace $C\subset Q_q^d$ with  distance $t$ is
   257	called $[d,k,t]$ code over $GF(q)$. By Proposition \ref{proNM5} we
   258	see that any $[d,d-t,t+1]$ code over $GF(q)$ is an $MDS(t,d,q)$
   259	code. By using a well-known construction of a linear MDS code (see
   260	\cite{MS}, Chapters 10,11, or \cite{Glock}, Theorem 9.1) by means of
   261	an appropriate parity-check matrix  over $GF(q)$ we can conclude
   262	that the following proposition is true.
   263	
   264	\begin{proposition}\label{proNMb}
   265	Let $q$ be a prime power. Then
   266	 for each  integers $d\leq q+1$  and $\varrho$,
   267	$3\leq \varrho < d$, there exists a linear (over $GF(q)$) {\rm MDS}
   268	code $C\subset Q_q^d$ with code distance $\varrho$.
   269	\end{proposition}
   270	
   271	
   272	
   273	
   274	We will say that an $MDS(t,d,q)$ code $M_0$ is a {\it super}
   275	$MDS(t,d,q)$ code if there exist $MDS(t+1,d,q)$ code $M_1$ and
   276	$MDS(t+2,d,q)$ code $M_2$ such that $M_2\subset M_1\subset M_0$.
   277	
   278	
   279	By removal of any row from a parity-check matrix of a linear {\rm
   280	MDS} code with distance $t+1$, we obtain a parity-check matrix of an
   281	{\rm MDS} code with distance $t$ that contains the original code.
   282	Thus Propositions \ref{proNMbb} and \ref{proNMbc} follow from
   283	Proposition \ref{proNMb}.
   284	
   285	
   286	\begin{proposition}\label{proNMbb}
   287	Let $q$ be a prime power. Then
   288	 for each  integers $d\leq q+1$  and $\varrho$,
   289	$3\leq \varrho < d-2$, there exists a linear over $GF(q)$ super {\rm
   290	MDS} code $C\subset Q_q^d$ with code distance $\varrho$.
   291	\end{proposition}
   292	
   293	\begin{proposition}\label{proNMbc}
   294	Let $q$ be a prime power. Then
   295	 for each  integers $d\leq q+1$  and $\varrho$,
   296	$3\leq \varrho < d-1$, there exists a linear over $GF(q)$  {\rm MDS}
   297	code $C\subset Q_q^d$ with code distance $\varrho$ that is an union
   298	of $q$ disjoint linear over $GF(q)$  {\rm MDS} code $C\subset Q_q^d$
   299	with code distance $\varrho+1$.
   300	\end{proposition}
   301	
   302	
   303	
   304	The set $Q_{q_1q_2}$ can be considered as the Cartesian product
   305	$Q_{q_1}\times Q_{q_2}$. Consequently, we can identify
   306	$Q^d_{q_1}\times Q^d_{q_2}$ and the hypercube $Q^d_{q_1q_2}$. Thus
   307	if $C_1\subset Q^d_{q_1}$ and $C_2\subset Q^d_{q_2}$ then
   308	$$C_1\times C_2=\{((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2),\dots,(x_d,y_d)) :
   309	(x_1,\dots,x_d)\in C_1, (y_1,\dots,y_d)\in C_2\}\subset
   310	Q^d_{q_1q_2}.$$
   311	
   312	\begin{proposition}[McNeish]\label{proNMb1}
   313	Suppose  $M_1$ is an (super) $MDS(t,d,q_1)$ code and $M_2$ is an
   314	(super) $MDS(t,d,q_2)$ code. Then $M_1\times M_2$ is  an (super)
   315	$MDS(t,d,q_1q_2)$ code.
   316	\end{proposition}
   317	
   318	By combining results of Propositions \ref{proNMb} and \ref{proNMb1}
   319	we obtain that  $MDS(2,5,q)$ codes  exist if
   320	$q=2^{\delta_2}3^{\delta_3}5^{\delta_5}\dots$, where $\delta_2\neq
   321	1$ and $\delta_3\neq 1$.
   322	
   323	Let $A\subset Q_q$. Denote by $\pi_A$ a function mapping from
   324	$Q_p\times Q_q$ to $Q_{p(q-|A|)+|A|}$ by the following rule:
   325	$\pi_A(x,y)=(x,y)$ if $y\not \in A$, and $\pi_A(x,y)=y$ if $y\in A$.
   326	Let $C_1\subset Q^d_{p}$ and $C_2\subset Q^d_{q}$. Denote
   327	$C_1\times_A C_2=\{(\pi_A(z_1),\dots,\pi_A(z_d)) : \overline{z}\in
   328	C_1\times C_2\}$.
   329	%A set $H$ is called a $j$-$A$-hole of $Q^d_q$ if it consist of all
   330	%vectors are contained at least $j$ coordinates from $A$.
   331	For any $C\subset Q^d_q$ we denote by $U_t(C)$  the $t$-neighborhood
   332	of $C$, i.\,e., $U_t(C)=\{x\in Q^d_q : \exists y\in C, \rho(x,y)\leq
   333	t\}$.
   334	
   335	%A $t$-neighborhood of $j$-$A$-hole is a $(j-t)$-$A$-hole.
   336	
   337	A set $D\subset Q^d_q$ is called an $MDS(t,d,q)$ with $j$-$A$-hole
   338	($t+1\leq j\leq d$) if\\ 1) the code distance of $D$ is equal to $t+1$;\\
   339	2) $D\cap U_{d-j+t}(A^d)=\varnothing$;\\ 3)
   340	$U_{d-j+t}(D)=Q^d_q\backslash A^d$;\\ 4)
   341	$|D|=\sum\limits_{k=0}^{j-t-1}{d-t \choose k}(q-|A|)^{d-t-k}|A|^k$.
   342	
   343	For $t=2$ and $d=5$ we get that an $MDS(2,5,q)$ code with
   344	$5$-$A$-hole has cardinality $q^3 -|A|^3$ and an $MDS(2,5,q)$ code
   345	with $4$-$A$-hole has cardinality $(q-|A|)^3 +3(q-|A|)^2|A|$.
   346	
   347	Suppose that $M$ is an $MDS(t,d,q)$ code, $a\in Q_q$,  and
   348	$\overline{a}=(a,\dots,a)\in M$. It is easy to see that $M\setminus
   349	\{\overline{a}\}$ is an $MDS(t,d,q)$ with $d$-$\{a\}$-hole. Let
   350	$A\subset Q_q$ and let $M$ be an MDS code, $M\subset Q_q^d$. A
   351	subset $M\cap A^d$   is called a {\it subcode}  of  $M$ if it is an
   352	MDS code  in $A^d$ with the same code distance as $M$. If $M\cap
   353	A^d$ is a subcode, then $M\setminus A^d$ is an $MDS(t,d,q)$ with
   354	$d$-$A$-hole.
   355	
   356	Let us formulate a known construction of a POLS (see \cite{DK},
   357	Chapter 4) in introduced terms.
   358	
   359	\begin{proposition}\label{theoMOLS0}
   360	Suppose that
   361	\begin{itemize} \item $ M_1\subset M$ is
   362	$MDS(3,4,p)$ code and $M$ is $MDS(2,4,p)$ code, \item $D$ is an
   363	$MDS(2,4,q)$ code,
   364	\item $E$ is an $MDS(2,4,q_1-q)$ code on alphabet $A$,\item $F$ is
   365	an $MDS(2,4,q_1)$ code with $4$-$A$-hole, where $|A|=q_1-q$.
   366	\end{itemize}
   367	 Then
   368	 the set $C=E\cup (M_1\times_A F) \cup
   369	((M\setminus M_1)\times D)$ is an $MDS(2,4,(p-1)q+q_1)$ code.
   370	\end{proposition}
   371	
